    "ls|LS16_S15_U_251": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.251 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2018 \n \nBARRING ALIEN COUNTRIES FROM PARTICIPATION  \nIN POWER TRANSMISSION PROJECTS \n \n251.  SHRI B. SENGUTTUVAN: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Ministry is likely to  bar the Chinese power companies from  \nparticipation in power transmission  projects on the ground of growing security  \nconcerns and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b)  whether the Chinese Government bars the participation of foreign investors \nin electrical energy sector whilst India  allows 100% FDI in the field and if so, the  \ndetails thereof; and \n \n(c)  whether any policy decision has  been taken by the Ministry to disallow  \ncountries that do not offer reciprocal  investment in the field of power and to  \ndeclare them ineligible to invest in power  transmission projects in the country \nand if so, the details thereof? \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER AND \nNEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY  \n \n( SHRI  R. K. SINGH ) \n \n(a) :  There is no proposal under consideration in the Ministry to bar the \nChinese power companies from participation in power transmission projects on \nthe ground of growing security concerns. \n \n(b) :  Ministry of External Affairs have informed that as per 7th Revision to \nForeign Investment Industry Catalogue 2017 of Chinese Government, which \nregulates foreign investments in various sectors of the Chinese economy;  foreign \ninvestment in  sector related to energy/power generation is not prohibited. \nInvestment in p ower generation and supply is encouraged by the Chinese \nGovernment, though it doesn’t provide the detail on the share of ownership which \na foreign investor can hold.  However, “Construction and management of nuclear \npower plant”, “Construction and operation  of power grid”, form a part of the \n“Restricted List” in China according to which   the foreign investor mandatorily \nhas to form JV with Chinese company and the majority of share holding will be \nwith Chinese partner. \n \n(c) :   There is no policy decision tak en by the Ministry to disallow  \ncountries that do not offer reciprocal investment in the field of power and to \ndeclare them ineligible to invest in power transmission projects in the country.  \n \n**************** \n\n ",
    "ls|LS16_S15_U_252": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.252\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 3RD AUGUST, 2018\nPREVENTION OF ANAEMIA AMONG WOMEN\n†*252. SHRI HARISHCHANDRA CHAVAN:\nSHRIMATI RAMA DEVI:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) whether 65 per cent women in the country are suffering from anaemia and if so,\nthe details thereof;\n(b) the various steps taken by the Government for prevention of the said ailment;\n(c) the extent of success achieved by the Government in controlling anaemia as a\nresult of the said efforts along with the reaction of the Government thereto; and\n(d) the corrective measures taken by the Government in this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) to (d) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 252* FOR 3RD AUGUST, 2018\n(a) According to the National Family Health Survey (NFHS -4) 2015 -16, the\nprevalence of anaemia among women aged 15 -49 years is 53.0% . State/UT wise\nprevalence is Annexed.\n(b) The Government of India has been addressing the problem of anaemia, through\nNational Nutritional Anaemia Control Programme and more recently by adopting a\nlife cycle approach under the National Iron Plus Initiative (NIPI). The\ninterventions under NIPI incl ude age-appropriate preventive strategy of Iron and\nFolic Acid (IFA) supplementation for under five children (6 months - 5 yrs),\nchildren 6 -10 yrs, adolescents, pregnant women, and lactating mothers , as well as\na treatment protocol for facility -based management of anaemia. Apart from NIPI,\nthere are other programmes which address causes of anaemia other than iron\ndeficiency, such as National Deworming Day (NDD) for deworming, National\nVector Borne Disease Contro l Programme (NVBDCP) for prevention and control\nof malaria, special efforts to reach ou t to populations affected with\nhemoglobinopathies and National Programme for Prevention and Control of\nFluorosis (NPPCF).\n(c) There has been a decrease in anaemia preva lence in the different target\nbeneficiary groups over the last decade, between NFHS 3 (2005 -06) and NFHS 4\n(2015-16). Anaemia prevalence has reduced from 69.4% to 58.5% among children\nless than 5 years of age, 55.3% to 53.0% in women aged 15-49 years and 57.9% to\n50.3% in pregnant women.\n(d) The Government has recently released the “Anaemia Mukt Bharat” guidelines\nas a comprehensive strategy for preve ntion and control of anaemia, with a multi -\npronged approach and a more robust operational and accountability framework.\nThe strategy builds on the existing programme and also includes newer initiatives,\nsuch as:\n Better techniques for diagnosis and management of anaemia\n Strengthening of supply chain management\n Better convergence for effective implementation\n Increased focus on research needs around anaemia\n\nAnnexure\nState wise anaemia prevalence (%)  (NFHS-3, 2005-6 and NFHS- 4, 2015-16)\nStates Women in Reproductive age group (15-49 years)\nNFHS 3 NFHS 4\nAll India 55.3 53.0\nA& N Islands NA 65.7\nAndhra Pradesh 62.9 60.0\nArunachal Pradesh 50.5 40.3\nAssam 69.3 46.0\nBihar 67.4 60.3\nChandigarh NA 75.9\nChhattisgarh 57.5 47.0\nDadra & Nagar Haveli NA 79.5\nDaman & Diu NA 58.9\nDelhi 44.3 52.5\nGoa 38.0 31.3\nGujarat 55.3 54.9\nHaryana 56.1 62.7\nHimachal Pradesh 43.3 53.4\nJammu & Kashmir 52.0 40.3\nJharkhand 65.2 69.5\nKarnataka 51.2 44.8\nKerala 32.8 34.2\nLakshdweep NA 45.7\nMadhya Pradesh 55.9 52.5\nMaharashtra 48.4 48.0\nManipur 35.7 26.4\nMeghalaya 46.2 56.2\nMizoram 38.1 22.5\nNagaland NA 23.9\nOdisha 61.1 51.0\nPuducherry NA 52.4\nPunjab 38.0 53.5\nRajasthan 53.1 46.8\nSikkim 59.5 34.9\nTamil Nadu 53.2 55.1\nTelengana NA 56.7\nTripura 65.1 54.5\nUttar Pradesh 49.9 52.4\nUttarakhand 54.7 41.5\nWest Bengal 63.2 62.5",
    "ls|LS16_S15_U_253": "Government of India\nMinistry of Finance \nDepartment of Financial Services \nLOK SABHA\nStarred Question No. †*253\nTo be answered on Friday, August 3, 2018/Shravana 12, 1940 (Saka) \nFinancial Assistance to Rural Artisans\n†*253. SHRI SHARAD TRIPATHI: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development (NABARD) has \nprepared/proposes to formulate any scheme to provide financial assistance to the \nrural artisans of the country;\n (b) if so, the details of such schemes, State/UT-wise including Uttar Pradesh; \n(c) the details of the financial assistance extended by NABARD in this regard during \nthe last three years and the current year, scheme and State/UT-wise; and \n(d) the number of artisans benefited under various schemes funded by NABARD \nduring the said period, State/UT-wise?\nAnswer\nThe Minister of Finance\n(Shri Piyush Goyal)\n(a) to (d) : A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n\nStatement referred in reply to parts (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. *253 \nto be answered on 03.08.2018 regarding Financial Assistance to Rural Artisans by \nSHRI SHARAD TRIPATHI: \n(a) to (d): National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development (NABARD) has reported that \nthey supplement the various initiatives/schemes of the concerned Ministries of the Central \nGovernment by supporting and providing marketing platform to rural artisans and producers \nthrough various interventions/schemes as under:\n(i) Off-farm Producers Organisation (OFPOs): OFPOs scheme which was introduced \nin 2016-17 aims at formation and nurturing of Off-farm Producers Organisation (OFPOs) in \nclusters of off-farm sector activities to encourage collectivisation of producers which includes \nartisans. NABARD meets the resource requirements and guides the planned promotional \ninterventions in the formulation and nurturing of OFPOs. Under the scheme Financial \nAssistance in the form of grant, is available, (for intensive OFPOs having above 500 \nmembers) upto a maximum of Rs.175 lakhs for various interventions, which include \nprocurement of raw material; disseminating market information; dissemination of technology \nand innovations; facilitating finance for inputs; aggregation and storage of products; weaving, \nproduction of artifacts; brand building, packaging, labeling and standardization; quality \ncontrol; marketing and export.\nAs reported by NABARD, a total of 2005 artisans were benefitted with total amount \nsanctioned to the tune of Rs. 193.58 lakh in various States including Uttar Pradesh under the \nOFPOs scheme details of which are given in Annexure I.\n(ii)  Rural Mart: Rural Mart scheme, which was re-introduced during 2016-17 aims to \nprovide marketing linkages for artisans and manufacturers of handicrafts, village and cottage \nindustries and agro-based products. NABARD provides grant assistance to cover the running \ncosts of the Rural Mart, which includes rent, salary for sales person, publicity expenditure \nand provision of Solar LED bulbs/ lanterns, for a period of two years. The maximum grant \nassistance available for Rural Marts at block/taluka and district headquarters level is Rs.2.58 \nlakh and Rs.3.45 lakh respectively.  \nState-wise details of rural marts and amount released during 2016-17 & 2017-18, as reported \nby NABARD, is given in Annexure II\n(iii) Support for Exhibitions: NABARD supports various marketing events like \nexhibitions/ melas to provide a unique platform for artisans to exhibit and sell their products, \ndevelop linkages and interact with buyers directly. NABARD also sponsors participants from \nacross the country to take part in these events. \nState-wise details of the scheme including number of artisans benefitted and amount \nreleased during 2015-16 to 2017-18, as reported by NABARD, is given in  Annexure III.\n(iv)  Skill Development: NABARD supports eligible institutions including National Skill \nDevelopment Corporation affiliated Training Institutions, Govt. Agencies, RUDSETIs/RSETIs, \nCorporate Partners and NGOs/Vas in which artisans can also be the beneficiaries. \nState-wise details of the scheme including number of persons trained and amount released \nduring 2015-16 to 2017-18, as reported by NABARD, is given in  Annexure IV.\n\nAnnexure I to Lok Sabha SQ No. †*253 for 03.08.2018\nProjects sanctioned under Off-farm Producers Organization (OFPOs)\nS.No\nName of the State Date of sanction\nAmount \nsanctioned \n(Rs. in \nlakh)\nNumber \nof \nartisans \nbenefitte\nd \n1 Jammu & Kashmir 10.02.2015 7.53 250\n2 Jharkhand 31.03.2017 22.66 155\n3 Tamil Nadu 31.03.2017 23.24 350\n4 West Bengal 31.03.2017 22.66 250\n5 Uttar Pradesh 02.05.2017 22.66 250\n6 Mizoram 31.10.2017 47.63 250\n7 Chhattisgarh 28.02.2018 23.60 250\n8 Odisha 20.03.2018 23.60 250\n    Total 193.58 2005\nAll the above projects are \nin progress\nSource: NABARD\n\n(Amt. in Rs.)\nPhysical \n(No. of \nRural \nMarts)\nAmount \nreleased\nPhysical \n(No. of \nRural \nMarts)\nAmount \nreleased\n1 Andaman & Nicobar 3 180000 1 224000\n2 Andhra Pradesh 4 0 4 393050\n3 Arunachal Pradesh 3 138090 2 459500\n4 Assam 4 54250 2 318500\n5 Bihar 1 2 89750\n6 Chhattisgarh 1 0\n7 Gujarat 2 57700\n8 Haryana 2 58250\n9 Himachal Pradesh 1 0 3 266250\n10 Jammu & Kashmir 3 149700\n11 Jharkhand 2 133800\n12 Karnataka 4 0 2 432960\n13 Kerala 10 673200 3 2234500\n14 Madhya Pradesh 2 55750\n15 Manipur 1 57000\n16 Meghalaya 1 163000\n17 Mizoram 2 72000\n18 Odisha 4 40000 5 380000\n19 Punjab 16 95750 2 1886880\n20 Rajasthan 5 36250 4 412000\n21 Sikkim 2 187000\n22 Tamil Nadu 11 126250 2 1152730\n23 Telangana 2 0 2 150500\n24 Uttar Pradesh 3 6 571000\n25 Uttarakhand 3 4 627689\n26 West Bengal 2 627000 4 210000\nTOTAL 76 1970790 66 10743509\nSource: NABARD\nSince the scheme was re-introduced during 2016-17 the data for 2015-16 has \nnot been given.\nAnnexure II to Lok Sabha SQ No. *253 for 03.08.2018\nSr. \nNo.\n2