    "ls|LS16_S15_U_264": " \n      GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA  \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *264 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.08.2018 \n \nINDO-GERMAN SKILL DEVELOPMENT VENTURE/PARTNERSHIP \n \n*264. DR. KIRIT  P. SOLANKI: \n \n Will the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPR ENEURSHIP be pleased \nto state : \n  \n(a)  whether Indo-German skill development venture/ partnership has been signed in the \nrecent past and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the said venture/partnership is on trac k to achieve its targets and if so, the \ndetails of targets/objectives set and achievements made therefrom so far;  \n \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(d) whether the said partnership has resulted in sc aling up of apprenticeship training and if \nso, the details thereof? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \n \nMINISTER IN THE MINISTRY OF \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n  \n  \n\n-2- \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) of LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.264 TO BE ANSWERED ON 06.08.2018 ASKED BY DR. KIRIT P. \nSOLANKI REGARDING INDO-GERMAN SKILL VENTURE/PARTNERSHIP  \n(a)     Subsequent to transfer of two verticals of the Dire ctorate General of Employment and \nTraining (DGE&T) to the Ministry of Skill Developme nt and Entrepreneurship (MSDE), a new \nMemorandum of Understanding (MoU) for cooperation i n the field of “Skill Development and \nVocational Education and Training” (SD&VET) was sig ned between MSDE and the Federal \nMinistry of Education and Research (BMBF) and the F ederal Ministry for Economic Co-\noperation and Development (BMZ) of the Republic of Germany on 5 th  October, 2015. Thereafter, \nan Implementation Agreement on the Indo-German Prog ramme for Vocational Education and \nTraining (IGVET) was signed on 22 nd August 2016 between the Ministry of Skill Developme nt \nand Entrepreneurship and Deutsche Gesellschaft für Internationale Zusammenarbeit (GIZ) \nGmbH (executive agency for the German Government). IGVET is a part of the bilateral \ncooperation between the Government of India and the  German Federal Ministry for Economic \nCooperation and Development (BMZ). Further, one mor e collaboration viz Joint Declaration of \nIntent was signed between the BMBF and MSDE on 30.0 5.2017 on the advancement of Dual \nVET and collaboration on training of VET Cluster Ma nagers and Indian Skill Development \nOfficers. \n(b) & (c)    Yes Madam, programme is on track to ac hieve its objective. Stakeholders from the \nprivate and public sectors are working together to achieve conditions conducive to create and \nimprove cooperative, workplace-based Vocational Edu cation and Training (VET) in India. For \nachieving this objective, the project is working on the following targets:- \n1. Private sector stakeholders actively participate  in the process of designing the \ncooperative VET; \n2. Cooperative VET models are implemented in select ed sectors; \n3. Based on experiences of implementation reform pr oposals for cooperative VET at \nnational and states level are proposed; and \n4. The cooperative VET models and success stories a re disseminated pan-India for further \nreplication. \nAchievements so far:- \n•  In the three project clusters – automotive in Aura ngabad; Electronics and manufacturing \nin Bangalore; and energy efficient construction in Bhiwadi (Rajasthan) – selected industry \nassociations are actively participating in designin g cooperative VET offers meeting the \nrequirements of the companies to get qualified manp ower. The industrial associations \ntaking  active  part  in  this  development  are :  Confederation  on  Indian  Industries (CII)  \n \n \n\n-3- \nAurangabad Chapter, CII Women Network (CII-WN) Cham ber of Marathwada Industries \nand Agriculture (CMIA), Marathwada Association of S mall Scale Industries and \nAgriculture (MSSIA), Bengaluru Chamber of Industry and Commerce (BCIC), Electronics \nCity Industries Association (ELCIA), UPVC Doors and  Window Manufacturing \nAssociation (UWDMA) and Bhiwadi Manufacturers Association (BMA). \n•  5 Associations have established Skill Development Units (SDUs) responsible for \nmanaging the development of manpower for the cluste r members. This is the first major \nchange in having the private sector taking responsibility for the practical training of youth. \n•   The ambitions of some of the associations are hig h: the CMIA will develop a regional \necosystem for small and medium-sized enterprises. 3 6 companies have confirmed their \nreadiness to participate in cooperative training.  \n•  The UWDMA, Bhiwadi has developed a new optional tr ade under National \nApprenticeship Promotion Scheme (NAPS) for the trai ning of UPVC windows and door \nmakers. UWDMA has a Memorandum of Understanding (Mo U) with the Government of \nRajasthan to establish training centre at Industria l Training Institute (ITI) Bhiwadi, where \na vocational training centre financed by the member companies has been established.  \n•  2 MoUs for cooperation between Member Company of B CIC and ITIs have signed. \n•  The participation of private stakeholders in coope rative training has achieved an \nencouraging level of readiness and models of cooperative VET are evolving.  \n•  Industry clusters are establishing cooperative VET  roundtable with key public and private \nstakeholders for developing the needed partnership of both sides. \n(d) The cooperation between the private and public sectors is evolving; a systemic approach \nfor enabling Industry Associations to play a strong  role as skill service provider to the member \ncompanies is in process. Their commitment to share the responsibility for training of skilled \nmanpower is a major change in the VET system in Ind ia. The common understanding between \nthe Indian and German government is that the IGVET project presents ideas from the German \nDual system to be tested and adapted to the Indian context. Therefore, the project focuses \nstrongly on models that can be replicated and scale d-up throughout India with potential to scale \nup the apprenticeship training system in India. \n****** ",
    "ls|LS16_S15_U_265": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE\n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 265\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 6\nTH\n \nAUGUST, 2018\n \n \n \n \nCULTURAL DEVELOPMENT PROGRAMMES \n \n \n \n*265.   SHRI RAJESH KUMAR DIWAKER:\n \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nthe details o\nf zonal cultural centres operating in the country in each \nState, especially in UP and its Hathras region at present;\n \n(b)\n \nwhether the Government has taken any steps to promote cultural \ndevelopment programmes in various parts of the country including \nUttar P\nradesh and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)\n \nthe details of existing cultural development programmes in the \ncountry; \n \n(d)\n \nwhether the Government proposes to further promote cultural \ndevelopment programmes in various parts of the country, especially in \nthe s\naid region of UP; and \n \n(e)\n \nif so, the details thereof and the other steps taken/being taken by the \nGovernment to promote the culture of our country?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (I/C) FOR CULTURE\n \nAND \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST & CLIMATE CHAN\nGE \n \n \n \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA)\n \n \n \n(a) to (e)\n \nA Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n\n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRE\nD TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) \nTO \n(\ne\n) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. \n2\n65\n \nFOR \n6\nTH\n \nAUGUST, \n201\n8\n \n \n \n(a)\n \nTo protect, promote & preserve various forms of fo\nlk art and culture \nthroughout the country, the Government of India has set up seven Zonal Cultural \nCentres (ZCCs) with\n \ntheir \nheadquarters\n \nlocated\n \nat Patiala, Nagpur, Udaipur, \nAllahabad, Kolkata, Dimapur and Thanjavur.\n \nUttar Pradesh is \na\n \nmember State of Nor\nth \nCentral Zone Cultural Centre (NCZCC), Allahabad\n. \n \n \n(b\n) to (e)\n \nTo promote cultural development programmes in various parts of the \ncountry, these ZCCs undertake various cultural activities and programmes in their \nmember States including Uttar Pradesh.  \nEa\nch ZCC has a Programme Committee\n \nconsisting of representatives from its member States, \nwhich approves their annual \ncalendar of cultural activities to be carried out throughout the country. \nA s\ntatement \nindicating the details of various cultural activities/ \nprogrammes conducted by th\ne \nNCZCC\n, Allahabad\n \nduring the last 2 years\n \nin Uttar Pradesh is Annexed. \nFurther, \nt\nhe \nMinistry\n \norganizes Cultural Development Programmes like\n \nRashtriya Sanskriti \nMahotsavs\n \n(RSMs)\n \nin the country including Uttar Pradesh to create awa\nreness \namongst the youth about \nIndia’s rich cultural heritage. \nRSMs \nhave been organized \nat \nNew Delhi (November, 2015 and October, 2016), Varanasi (December, 2016), \nBengaluru & Tawang (March, 2017), Gujarat (October, 2017), Karnataka (January, \n2018) and Mad\nhya Pradesh (February, 2018). \nFurther, i\nt has also been decided to \norganize seven (7) RSMs at Delhi, Maharashtra (Mumbai), West Bengal (Kolkata), \nMadhya Pradesh, Uttar Pradesh, Odisha and Rajasthan during 2018\n-\n19.\n \n \nAll the ZCCs as \nalso other organizations \nunder Ministry of Culture will participate in the upcoming \nKumbh Mela at Allahabad.\n \n \n \nOctave, a festival of the North East is organized by\n \neach of the\n \n7\n \nZCC\ns\n \nto \npromote folk culture of the North Eastern States. For this purpose, artists of the \nNorth East r\negion are engaged\n \nby the \nrespective\n \nZCC\n \nto showcase\n \nand project the\n \nrich cultural heritage\n \nof North East\n \nat Pan India level. \n \n \nApart from the above, t\nhese \nZCCs \nalso\n \nconduct workshops, seminars, \nexhibitions, cultural fairs etc. on regular basis all over Ind\nia for promotion of Indian \nculture.     \n \n\n \n \nANNEXURE \n \n \n \nActivities/ programmes organized by NCZCC, Allahabad in \n \nUttar Pradesh during 2016\n-\n17\n \n \nSl. No.\n \nName of the activity/ programme\n \nDate\n \n1\n \nKavi Sammelan \n \nMar\n-\n16\n \n2\n \nSanjhi Virasat\n \nApr\n-\n16\n \n3\n \nCultural Progra\nm for Soldiers\n \nMay\n-\n16\n \n4\n \nKavya Kumbh\n \nMay\n-\n16\n \n5\n \nProduction oriented summer workshop\n \nMay/ June 2016\n \n6\n \nInternational Yoga Day\n \nJun\n-\n16\n \n7\n \nCultural Program\n \nJun\n-\n16\n \n8\n \nCultural Program\n \nJun\n-\n16\n \n9\n \nGreeshmotsav\n \nJun\n-\n16\n \n10\n \nJagarnath Mahotsav\n \nJul\n-\n16\n \n11\n \nCultural Progr\nam at Raj Bhawan\n \nAug\n-\n16\n \n12\n \nKajri Mela \n \nAug\n-\n16\n \n13\n \nFoundation Day\n \nSep\n-\n16\n \n14\n \nRamleela Mahotsav\n \nOct\n-\n16\n \n15\n \nCultural Program (RPF)\n \nOct\n-\n16\n \n16\n \nHanumat Janmotsav\n \nOct\n-\n16\n \n17\n \nHansvahini Mahotsav\n \nOct\n-\n16\n \n18\n \nRamayan Mela Shrigverpur\n \nOct\n-\n16\n \n19\n \nAntragini \n \nOct\n-\n16\n \n2\n0\n \nAmethi Mahotsav\n \nOct\n-\n16\n \n21\n \nKartik Poornima Mahotsav\n \nNov\n-\n16\n \n22\n \nChitrakoot Mela \n \nNov\n-\n16\n \n23\n \nKapil Vats Mahotsav\n \nDec\n-\n16\n \n24\n \nChalo Man Ganga Jamuna Teer 2017\n \nJan\n-\n17\n \n25\n \nUtrani Mela \n \nJan\n-\n17\n \n26\n \nCultural Program \n-\n \nIIIT \n \nFeb\n-\n17\n \n27\n \nCultural Program \n-\nAllahabad \nMuseum\n \nFeb\n-\n17\n \n28\n \nRamayan Mela \n \nFeb\n-\n17\n \n29\n \nAanapur Varshik Mahotsav\n \nFeb\n-\n17\n \n30\n \nGanesh Utsav \n \nFeb\n-\n17\n \n31\n \nBadayun Mahotsav\n \nFeb\n-\n17\n \n32\n \nOm Grinag Dham Mahotsav\n \nFeb\n-\n17\n \n33\n \nShivratri Mela Ghusarnath Dham\n \nFeb\n-\n17\n \n34\n \nShivratri Mela Bhayharan Nath Dham\n \nFeb\n-\n17\n \n35\n \nS\nhivratri Mela Belkhar Nath Dham\n \nFeb\n-\n17\n \n36\n \nGonda Mahotsav\n \nFeb\n-\n17\n \n37\n \nRashtriya Shilp Mela \n \nMar\n-\n17\n \n38\n \nRaj Gaddi Samaroh \n \nMar\n-\n17\n \n39\n \nThithauli (Kavi Sammelan)\n \nMar\n-\n17\n \n40\n \nMahakavi Nirala Kavi Sammelan\n \nMar\n-\n17\n \n41\n \nChirag \n-\nE\n-\nBismill Memorial Kavi